## Public contract (this is all you get — no implementation source)

```python
OPENAI_ENCODINGS: dict[str, str] = {
    "gpt-5": "o200k_base",
    "gpt-4o": "o200k_base",
    "gpt-4": "cl100k_base",
}


def count_openai(text: str, encoding: str) -> int:
    """Count `text`'s OpenAI tokens for `encoding`.

    `encoding` is a `tiktoken` encoding name ("o200k_base", "cl100k_base",
    ...), not a model name. It calls `tiktoken.get_encoding(encoding).encode(text)`
    internally and returns the length. No frame to subtract: this is an
    exact partition, additive across sections with zero drift.
    """
```

`tiktoken` is a real, installed dependency (`tiktoken>=0.14`) — you may call
`tiktoken.get_encoding(...)` directly in your tests to compute expected
values, since it is deterministic and exact (unlike ctok's Claude frame,
there is nothing to approximate here).

Behavior to test, one test (or more) per item:

- AC-01: for a non-trivial piece of text (a few sentences), calling
  `count_openai(text, "o200k_base")` returns exactly
  `len(tiktoken.get_encoding("o200k_base").encode(text))` — deterministic
  and exact, not just "some positive number".
- AC-02: additivity with zero drift. Split a non-trivial text into at least
  three parts (e.g. by sentence or paragraph) and verify that
  `sum(count_openai(part, "o200k_base") for part in parts)` equals
  `count_openai("".join(parts), "o200k_base")` EXACTLY — unlike Claude,
  there must be no discrepancy at all.
- AC-03: `count_openai(text, "cl100k_base")` must reproduce exactly what
  `tiktoken.encoding_for_model("gpt-4")` would give for that same text (both
  route to the same `cl100k_base` encoding) — assert the two counts are
  equal for a non-trivial text.
- AC-04: calling `count_openai(text, encoding)` with an `encoding` string
  that `tiktoken.get_encoding` does not recognize (e.g. `"not-a-real-encoding"`)
  raises an exception (any exception type — do not assert on a specific one).
- AC-05: `count_openai("", "o200k_base")` returns exactly `0` — unlike
  Claude's `count_claude`, there is no frame overhead to net out for OpenAI.

